WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.272 --> 00:00:02.521
- The prosecution rests.

00:00:02.521 --> 00:00:04.130
(gasps)
- That's it, on that?

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.050
He's a plaintiff's lawyer.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.820
He's trying to get damages on behalf

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.280
of his client the snowboarder.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.280
- Two script errors here, major.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.920
- That's like getting your name wrong.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.232
(laughs)

00:00:13.232 --> 00:00:16.400
(playful music)

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.060
Hey, Legal Eagles, by popular demand,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.440
I'm finally tackling a medical drama

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.870
that involves malpractice, but to do that,

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.070
I think I'm going to need
a medical consultation.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.670
Doctor.
(twinkles)

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.030
We're ready for surgery.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.217
Let's dig in.

00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:33.967
(playful music)

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.473
- Are we losing this case?

00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:39.400
- It's too early to panic.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.810
We haven't presented our side yet.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:41.680
- There she is.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.500
- You haven't presented anything.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.580
- Where was his opening statement?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.150
- Where was this opening statement?

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.880
Where were all the
questions that he was asking

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.100
of the other witnesses?

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.820
- Don't they, like after the witness

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.240
presents for the plaintiff,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.620
right away the defense
gets to speak to them?

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.850
- Yes, well, there's two ways
of doing an opening statement.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.220
You can do what's called
reserving the opening statement,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.970
where instead of giving your
opening statement immediately

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.550
after the plaintiff, you
wait until the plaintiff

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.840
has presented their whole case

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.170
and then at the start of your case,

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.470
then you give your own opening statement.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.303
- Oh, okay.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.310
- And there are strategic
reasons why you might do that.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.680
In a case like this, you would 100%--

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.650
- [Dr. Mike] Want to go right after.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.450
- Get right in there,
explain all the things

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.190
that the plaintiff did not explain to you,

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.150
and get your theme out
there, which is that

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.820
this is a bad thing that
happened to a good kid.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.850
It's not a matter of medical negligence.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.920
It's just that all of these complications

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.200
that no one could have foreseen happened,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.080
caused this whole cascade of things.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.100
But the doctors did everything right.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.450
- His legs were black.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.550
I knew it wasn't good.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.350
They cut them off that night.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.410
- So this is the plaintiff's girlfriend.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.730
She would have been a great
witness to start things off.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.960
- Telling the story, emotional, sad.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.530
- No medical stuff whatsoever,

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.400
and whatever she witnessed firsthand,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.430
which is going to be
colored by a layperson.

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:56.120
It's going to seem really
horrible based on what she saw.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.853
That would be a great way
to get your story out there.

00:01:57.853 --> 00:02:00.220
- Did you have any
indication that Dr. Torres

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.478
was even worried about
something like amputation?

00:02:02.478 --> 00:02:04.710
I mean, did she ever mentioned
that it might be even a risk?

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.570
- [Girlfriend] No. She
was worried about it.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.541
She was worried about what it
would do to her reputation.

00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:11.914
- What is she talking about?

00:02:11.914 --> 00:02:12.870
- [Lawyer] Why would you say that?

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.703
- I have no idea.

00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:16.120
- I heard her talking in the hallway.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.540
She didn't know I could hear her.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.110
- [Lawyer] What did she say?
(tense music)

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.620
- She said "I can't be the doctor

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.853
who turns an Olympic
athlete into a doorstop."

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:26.830
- Is that hearsay? That
sounds hearsay-ish.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.270
- That is a, that is exactly
an example of hearsay.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.430
However, there is an
exception to the hearsay rule

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.920
in the federal rules that would be 801d2,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:35.930
federal rules of evidence,

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.370
that is an admission by a party opponent.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.670
So this is the plaintiff
using the statement

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.480
of the defendant against them.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.870
Arguably, this is an
admission by a party opponent

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.310
or a declaration against interest.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.780
It's also being used for
its non-hearsay value

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.330
in terms of she's literally
turning someone into a doorstep

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:53.550
which is incredibly un-PC.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.300
I can't believe they
included that in here,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.880
but also that she was
flustered, she was worried.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.730
She is partially admitting fault.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.010
So it sort of informs all
of her downstream actions.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.720
- Can you get off of that
thing for five seconds

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.553
and look at this place?

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:08.138
- Yeah, I know, hold on a second.

00:03:08.138 --> 00:03:09.340
I'm just, I'm following
up on that clinical trial

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.810
in Germany using Peterson hip joints.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.430
I'm trying to get them
to send me their data.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.490
- Well, they're not
going to release anything

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:14.970
until it's published.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:15.803
- Sometimes they will.

00:03:15.803 --> 00:03:17.530
It's called the pre-print publication.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.230
- Is that the kind of research
that an orthopedic surgeon

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.439
would do before doing
surgery for the first time?

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:27.280
Well, using a hip that
she's never used before?

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.830
- It should be, (laughs)

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.090
because you want to know
what the implications

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.230
of using the joint would be,
what side effects patients had,

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.540
especially if it's different

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.480
than the research that already exists.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.450
- I would expect that a
surgeon at the top of her field

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.610
would research clinical trials.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.220
She would examine whatever publications

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.650
the manufacturer has put out.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:49.520
- It's not like there's
hundreds of studies

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.353
that they have to go through.

00:03:50.353 --> 00:03:52.280
There's like a handful
that are good quality.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.940
So like two or three
studies that you go through

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:54.920
and you know them inside out,

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.260
you know what the complications are,

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.010
and you present it to the patient

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.730
getting them to understand
what the risks would be.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.160
And again, that's not the
core of the problem here.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.370
The core of the problem is how she acted

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.510
after the procedure, so like,
that's the real issue here.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.523
I don't think the joint
is even the problem.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:10.670
- There, sent.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:12.290
- Oh, that's it? That's
how she does her research?

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.090
Like, Oh, okay, now let's show me this.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.150
- I sent a text.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:15.983
- Yeah.

00:04:15.983 --> 00:04:16.960
- Try to explain that to the jury.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.880
You have to believe me,
I did everything I could.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.950
- Look at this text. I DMed the author.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.360
- That actually would've
come out in evidence.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.193
- [Dr. Mike] Really?

00:04:24.193 --> 00:04:25.330
- Oh yeah, there would
be so much discovery

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.330
and document requests,
and you would absolutely

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.980
look for electronic
documents, including emails,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.110
texts, anything that references
the hip, Travis Reed,

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.070
the snowboarder, you'd ask for
all of that from the doctor.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.900
And it would have been turned
over to the other side.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.880
- So for example, the
plaintiff can request

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.270
personal emails as well?

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.370
- Oh, absolutely.
- Wow.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.550
- That's really the bread and butter

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.660
of litigation these days.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.540
People are relatively smart
in terms of not saying things

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:48.373
that they shouldn't say,

00:04:48.373 --> 00:04:50.050
or at least not having a record of it.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.350
People say all kinds of
crazy stuff in emails.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.500
- Hey Travis.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.333
- Hey.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.513
- You know, I'm thinking
about snowboarding.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.450
In fact, I've picked up a board,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.401
a Burton blunt, are you
familiar with that one?

00:05:04.401 --> 00:05:05.234
- [Travis] It's a good board.

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:07.500
- No, you can't ask these questions.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.830
- There's better boards for beginners.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.499
What size shoe are you?

00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:11.567
- I'm a 13.

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.610
- You might want to
look into an extra wide.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.276
- Object.
- Don't hang over too much.

00:05:15.276 --> 00:05:17.890
A little overhang's fine, but
too much is gonna cause drag.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.050
- [Lawyer] And drag's bad?

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.060
- Objection, your honor.
Where is this going?

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.240
- Objection, where is this going?

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.110
That is not an objection recognized

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.230
by the federal rules of evidence.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.070
You'd be objecting from
the very start of this.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.159
This is not relevant.

00:05:30.159 --> 00:05:33.960
I get it, you're trying
to humanize your client,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.240
but the way you would
do that is establishing

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.080
the background that is actually relevant

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.550
to his economic and non-economic damages.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.383
- A hundred percent.

00:05:43.383 --> 00:05:45.330
- He's not able to snowboard now,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.390
so the obvious form of damages would be

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.600
the lost income that he
would have been able to make.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.100
And that would be a huge
part of this case too,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.260
in terms of disputing
whether he would even be able

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.360
to snowboard in the future.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.148
Because remember he started out
this episode with arthritis,

00:05:59.148 --> 00:06:02.980
arthritis so severe that
he couldn't land the trick

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.290
and he needed a replacement hip.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.063
That's one of the reasons
why you might bifurcate

00:06:07.063 --> 00:06:09.850
a trial like this, which
means that half of the trial

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.780
is about establishing
liability, who was negligent,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.410
which parties were negligent,
doctor, hospital, et cetera?

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:18.930
And then only once you have
established the relative fault.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.750
So you can have, this person
is responsible for 10%.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.820
This person's responsible for 50%.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.130
Once you've established that,

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.870
then you would go forward into damages.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.310
And then there would be a huge battle

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.310
where the defense would say,
"maybe we were negligent,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.790
but this poor kid was never going to

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.410
be able to snowboard again anyway,

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.630
therefore we don't owe damages

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.060
in the form of his lost income."

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.690
- Is it true that Dr. Torres
had never actually done

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.450
an operation with this
kind of joint before?

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:44.800
- Objection, speculation.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.730
He knows that because she told him.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.790
That's an example where
probably the hearsay

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.840
wouldn't come in, it's sort of speculation

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.650
as to whether that is in fact true.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:53.490
- True, okay.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:54.323
- That's true.

00:06:54.323 --> 00:06:55.156
- But you went ahead with it anyway?

00:06:55.156 --> 00:06:57.010
- She said she had done a lot of research.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:57.980
She said she was confident.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.620
- So you trusted her to use her expertise

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.530
to advise you and to keep you safe,

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.430
when in fact what you got

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.780
was a botched surgery and infection.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.780
- This is a leading question,
an extremely leading question.

00:07:13.922 --> 00:07:15.620
- I mean, look at you now.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.290
- That's not a question.

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.610
You don't get to pontificate
from the well of the court.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.410
The main thing is you have
to ask actual questions

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:24.430
in an examination.

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.030
- These aren't actual questions.

00:07:27.685 --> 00:07:29.685
- There's nothing there.

00:07:32.871 --> 00:07:34.400
(door slides)

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.050
- I'm really interested to
see how she handles this,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.130
because I think situations
like this decide

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.820
whether or not a patient is
actually going to continue.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.530
If you come in and you take
some of the fault yourself

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.220
and you actually say "sorry,"

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.620
statistics show less likely
for a lawsuit to happen.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.453
- Interesting.

00:07:51.453 --> 00:07:54.330
- But doctors, I don't know if
this is like older training,

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.810
an ego thing, what is it, but
they're afraid to say sorry

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.010
and say "we made a mistake."

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.103
And as a result, more, like
it looks worse to the patient,

00:08:02.103 --> 00:08:03.810
like the doctor doesn't care.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.130
They're not going to fix this.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.700
Other patients are going
to get hurt by this.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.310
Whereas if you apologize,
point out what you did wrong,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.640
point out what you learned,
how you're changing the system

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.560
so it never happens again, less,

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:14.800
it doesn't protect you obviously,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.180
but it gives that honesty to the patient

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.830
where they're less likely
to proceed as litigation.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.800
- Well, I would assume
that doctors are worried

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.610
about getting sued and admitting fault.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.310
So that is a reason why they wouldn't.

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:26.220
- But isn't it interesting
that it's actually backwards?

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.610
Something similar happens when
you're questioning patients.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.880
Something that med students
are always hesitant to ask is

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.900
if patients are thinking
about committing suicide

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.130
or hurting themselves,

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:35.763
and students, I don't want to ask that.

00:08:35.763 --> 00:08:37.920
What if it actually leads them to do that?

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.020
No such evidence.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.720
Asking that question
does not offend people.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.720
It does not cause more rates of suicide,

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.180
and it's actually reassuring

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.500
and you can get a lot of
information that could actually

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.150
help you protect the patient
from committing suicide.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.020
- Yeah, I imagine that's
one of the reasons why

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:53.450
empirical evidence-based
medicine is so important.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.420
- Exactly.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:55.253
- Because everyone has these hunches,

00:08:55.253 --> 00:08:57.360
but sometimes it's not
born out in the data.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.350
- Exactly.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.933
- It's Dr. Torres's fault.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:01.990
- Improper opinion.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.290
Objection, move to strike.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:05.440
- You feel very strongly about that.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:06.763
- It's not just a feeling.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.340
I know it, she told me.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:10.850
- [Lawyer] She admitted
that she was at fault?

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:11.720
- [Travis] Yes, she did.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:12.840
- I'm curious about this.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.210
- [Travis] The day she cut off my legs,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.740
she came into my room, she'd been crying.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.070
She told me she was sorry.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:19.040
- Objection.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.100
It is illegal to use a physician's apology

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.670
against them as evidence of guilt.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:25.650
- Your honor, this
isn't about the apology.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.972
This is about everything else
she said with the apology.

00:09:28.972 --> 00:09:30.410
- [Judge] I'll allow it.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:31.243
- Oh!

00:09:31.243 --> 00:09:33.410
- Okay, so big problems here.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.620
- Okay. I hope so.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.100
- It is in fact, the case
that there are about 40 states

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.000
in the US that have what
are called "I'm sorry" laws,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.850
which as you just talked
about, allow the facilitation

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.850
of doctors to apologize to a patient.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:49.190
In those states, it is illegal to use

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:52.359
a doctor's apology against
them in court like this.

00:09:52.359 --> 00:09:53.910
There are limits to that though.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.500
If the doctor goes
further than apologizing

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.727
into admitting fault,
then that is a statement

00:09:59.727 --> 00:10:01.980
that's not covered by the "I'm sorry" laws

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:03.736
and can be admissible.

00:10:03.736 --> 00:10:04.569
- [Travis] She said if there was

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:06.543
anyone to blame, it was her.

00:10:07.871 --> 00:10:09.870
She said this never should have happened.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:10.840
She said if there was any way

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.270
she could change things, she would.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.570
- [Lawyer] Those were her exact words?

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.140
- Yeah, I don't think that's
really admitting fault.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:21.063
- Yes they were.
(tense music)

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.098
- The prosecution rests.

00:10:27.098 --> 00:10:29.000
(gasps)
- That's it? On that?

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.420
- No, that's not the issue.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.560
He's the plaintiff's lawyer.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.800
The prosecution is a criminal thing.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.900
He's saying that he represents the state

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:39.660
and is prosecuting someone
for criminal guilt.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:40.610
He's a plaintiff's lawyer.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:41.850
He's trying to get damages

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.300
on behalf of his client, the snowboarder.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.820
- Two script errors here, major.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:49.060
That, and then them saying
postoperative infections

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:51.190
are common in the middle of surgery,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:53.300
when he didn't even
have his infection yet.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:54.709
- That's like getting your name wrong.

00:10:54.709 --> 00:10:56.250
(laughs)

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.313
- And the surgery, it went smoothly?

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:03.130
- The cut position and the
femoral resurfacing were perfect.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.280
- What wasn't perfect?

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:07.060
- Well, at the end of
surgery, the patient desatted,

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:09.430
which means his oxygen level went down

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.010
and his heart was beating really fast,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.120
which is really dangerous.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:14.860
We thought he might have
a clot in his lungs,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.422
so it was vital that we
finished the surgery quickly.

00:11:17.422 --> 00:11:18.799
- And that's when he left the sponge?

00:11:18.799 --> 00:11:21.300
- Yes. And I would do it again.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.730
- So this is actually really
good direct examination.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:24.876
- Yeah, I agree.

00:11:24.876 --> 00:11:28.130
- And the defense is
actually putting their main

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.680
witness first, which is
probably the right way to go.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.700
It's either that or go with an expert.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.370
It's sort of crazy that the plaintiff

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.033
did not have an expert.

00:11:37.033 --> 00:11:40.240
You would have multiple
experts in a suit like this.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.050
It would be a battle of the experts.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:44.730
- Well, the patient's life was at risk.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.080
I believe that the sponge
wouldn't compromise the joint.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.543
I made a call and I was right.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:51.230
It was the right call.

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:54.660
- So leaving the sponge was
deliberate, not a mistake?

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.420
You weren't being careless or distracted.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.830
- I mean, I think at this
point, we haven't seen anything

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:01.340
that's related to the sponge at all.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:03.280
We actually never heard
about the sponge again.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.210
- What does a sponge have
to do with him getting

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.700
an infection in his heart,
leading to septic emboli

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:11.480
going to his legs and losing his legs?

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:15.800
Unless the sponge caused
an infection in the hip

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.556
and then because of
that sponge being there,

00:12:18.556 --> 00:12:21.800
then his hip was infected,
then his heart was infected,

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.750
and then he threw the clots.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:25.720
- We have to assume that
they've left the sponge in

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:29.110
for weeks because he went
home, came back with a fever.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:31.060
- Yeah.
- So he still has the sponge?

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.470
- If they thought there was a sponge,

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.340
but they needed to close
to make sure he wasn't

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:37.320
having a pulmonary embolism,
they close, they check.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.840
He's not having a pulmonary embolism.

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:40.910
They then they do the x-ray
to see if there's a sponge

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.380
in fact in there, why did they
send them home with a sponge?

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.070
- Well, they might've
been incredibly negligent.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:46.910
- Well then that's like,
that's not her fault.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:48.370
Then it's a hospital issue.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:50.560
- Make sure his BP and
O2 stats stay stable

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:51.760
and bring me as post-op x-ray.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.010
I want to locate that sponge.

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.407
- See?
- Yeah, I'm on team sponge.

00:12:55.407 --> 00:12:57.400
- It's my job to prioritize my patient

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.440
and that's exactly what I did.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:00.590
I wasn't focused on anything

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:03.283
or anyone other than my patient.

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.990
- [Lawyer] Let's talk
about this embolectomy.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.300
- What happened to the post-op sponge?

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.272
- I guess we'll never know.

00:13:11.272 --> 00:13:12.105
- You know, it's funny,

00:13:12.105 --> 00:13:13.160
in the beginning of the opening statement,

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:14.960
he said there's one crucial mistake.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:15.940
- Right.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:16.830
- And I can't wrap my
head around what the,

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:17.860
there's so many mistakes,

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.060
I don't know which one is
the one they're going after.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:21.910
- It's a cornucopia of medical mistakes.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:23.200
- Yeah, exactly.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.220
- Talk about this embolectomy.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.860
Dr. Yang wanted to do
the heart surgery first.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:30.180
You wanted to work on Travis's leg.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.013
- Yes.

00:13:31.013 --> 00:13:31.960
- [Lawyer] Why?

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:34.290
- Well, his foot was turning gray.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.123
- Oh, okay.

00:13:35.123 --> 00:13:37.650
So this doctor is saying
that she wasn't distracted

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.850
and we know that there
were things going on

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.820
that obviously effected her judgment.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.910
This would have come out in
the deposition, presumably.

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:45.570
The other attorney would have asked

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:46.900
what was going on that day.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:48.660
Were you distracted in any way?

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.830
And they might know the
information that she's withholding

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.060
from the jury here, and if you do that,

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:55.890
it's the absolute worst thing you can do

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.440
because true, it might
be an ancillary thing.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.030
- [Dr. Mike] But to the
jury, you look awful.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:01.450
- You have lied in front of the jury

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.290
and now your credibility is toast

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.260
and you are going to be found liable.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:08.282
- Well, his foot was turning
gray, which is an indication

00:14:08.282 --> 00:14:10.845
that the limb is starting to die.

00:14:10.845 --> 00:14:12.883
And I wanted to restore blood flow.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.160
Once a limb turns black,
there's nothing we can do.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:20.440
- As a vascular surgeon, you
are an orthopedic surgeon.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:21.950
- Could she have dual specialties?

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.700
- Oh, I, no, they're not even related.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.560
Maybe like a vascular cardiothoracic.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.250
You see how you could be
like a vascular surgeon

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.620
that works on the lower body

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:33.060
as well as someone who works in the chest,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:36.700
a dual board certification
in orthopedic and vascular.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:37.830
I've never heard of that.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:39.560
- [Lawyer] She knew that the heart

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:41.800
would keep throwing out
clots until it was repaired--

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:42.743
- Do you cook?

00:14:45.466 --> 00:14:46.383
- A little.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:49.290
- Okay, let's say you're cooking

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.503
and you start a grease
fire in your kitchen,

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:53.723
and then it spreads to your curtains.

00:14:53.723 --> 00:14:55.640
- Is she questioning her own counsel?

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:57.220
- I mean, you can, it's rhetorical.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:58.433
You can get away with this.

00:14:58.433 --> 00:14:59.266
- Okay.

00:14:59.266 --> 00:15:01.100
- What do you do? Do you
put out the grease fire

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:02.650
or do you extinguish the curtains?

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:06.010
Which disaster do you tackle first?

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:07.830
- It's so cynical of these writers.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:09.293
Oh, she's she's talking in terms

00:15:09.293 --> 00:15:11.190
that the jury can understand.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:12.050
I don't understand a blood clot.

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:13.760
- I can't follow the analogy though.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.200
- They needed to tackle
the worst thing first.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.010
- But was that the worst thing?

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:19.843
- Probably not.

00:15:19.843 --> 00:15:21.410
- The fact that the heart
is throwing out clots

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.470
all over the place and it'll
continue throwing out clots

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:24.370
whether or not you fix the leg.

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:25.580
- It's not a perfect metaphor.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.413
- Yeah.

00:15:26.413 --> 00:15:29.400
- If I can't feel for my
patients without getting sued,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.080
then I guess I'm just going
to keep on getting sued

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.795
because that's who I am.

00:15:32.795 --> 00:15:34.650
- I mean,
(laughs)

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.920
I don't think I would
want my client to say

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:39.521
that she's going to be
sued multiple times.

00:15:39.521 --> 00:15:41.240
This is one of those times where

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.030
maybe she's lapsing into a narrative,

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:44.570
which technically you're
not supposed to do.

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.210
But if I was the plaintiff's lawyer,

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:47.830
I don't want to come off as overbearing

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:49.270
and argumentative and objecting,

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.590
even if I'm right on a
ticky-tack objection.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:52.570
You just let it go.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.993
You let her tell her story
and let things move along.

00:15:57.707 --> 00:16:02.010
- I think it's from a clinical
trial on the Peterson hip.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:04.071
I thought I never heard back.

00:16:04.071 --> 00:16:04.970
- I thought I never heard back.

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:06.410
Well, you probably should
have followed up on that.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.660
- Enclosed is the data you
requested, but please be advised

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:10.500
we've currently put a stop to our trial.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.570
There have been five
instances of infection

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.183
caused by the pseudomonas bacteria.

00:16:15.183 --> 00:16:16.016
- What?

00:16:16.016 --> 00:16:18.110
- We've traced to source
back to the Peterson joint.

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.290
- Wait, so it wasn't the sponge?

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:22.550
- No, apparently it was
the hip manufacturer.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.200
- So why do they keep
talking about the sponge

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:25.530
if it was never in the patient?

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.520
- I don't think he's a good lawyer.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:29.810
- That's such a problem.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.400
So they closed, worry
that there was a sponge.

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:33.880
There was in fact, no sponge,

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.520
and yet they're still
discussing it in the court?

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.220
- It's a big swing and a miss.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:38.140
- [Torres] I can't just sit on this.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:38.973
I have to tell them, right?

00:16:38.973 --> 00:16:41.940
- I wouldn't. You didn't
willfully ignore any doubt.

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:44.510
You didn't act irresponsibly
or maliciously.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:47.410
You worked off the information
you had at the time.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:48.243
- Well, okay.

00:16:48.243 --> 00:16:49.320
- Act of God, I like that word.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:52.220
- So, okay, so that is technically true.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:54.580
She acted on the information
she had at the time.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.180
So in that sense, she
wasn't actively reckless.

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.100
She didn't know that it was,
I guess, a recalled hip.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:01.790
- The thing is if you get the hip,

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:04.170
in order to get it, you would
have known about the recall.

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:05.593
- Right, exactly, yeah.

00:17:05.593 --> 00:17:07.420
- It's not like you'd get it off eBay.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:10.390
- If this clinical trial is
having multiple instances

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:12.630
of this infection, it's a defective hip.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.810
I'm sure Peterson has recalled this hip.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:16.833
If she didn't check to see
if that thing was recalled.

00:17:16.833 --> 00:17:19.100
- No, you can't get it, you
physically can't get the hip.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:21.217
It's not like they send it
to you and they're like,

00:17:21.217 --> 00:17:22.750
"well, you should check if it's recalled."

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:24.140
No, you're ordering it
from the manufacturer.

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:26.430
- Caveat emptor, buyer beware.

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:27.560
- The only thing is that they could have

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:30.920
pre-ordered the hip, got
it before the recall,

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:31.860
then the recall happened.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.633
- Possibly, there's a lot
of negligence going on here.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.513
- Hey.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.693
Okay, thanks.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:42.970
They've reached a verdict.

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:44.100
- That's quick.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.490
- Well, we have no idea where
we are in time right now.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:50.730
So it's not uncommon to engage
in settlement discussions

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:53.010
with the other side,
right up until the time

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.017
the jury actually announces the verdict.

00:17:55.017 --> 00:17:55.850
- [Dr. Mike] Really?

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:56.683
- Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:17:56.683 --> 00:17:57.700
Because at that point--

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:58.690
- People get nervous.

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:00.720
- People get nervous,
all the evidence is out,

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:02.430
you know how the trial went.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.850
So you hear about people
settling on the courtroom steps,

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:06.683
happens all the time.

00:18:06.683 --> 00:18:07.890
- [Dr. Mike] Wow.
- Happens all the time.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.760
I've literally written out a
settlement in bullet points

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.310
on a legal pad, just
transcribing in the hallway

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:15.143
sign both sides.

00:18:15.143 --> 00:18:16.780
It's kind of like, kind of
like a letter of intent.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:18.220
- Wow, so then at that point,

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:19.910
do you stop the jury negotiations?

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:21.647
- Yep, you just go in
front of the judge and say,

00:18:21.647 --> 00:18:23.210
"your honor, we don't
need to hear the verdict.

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:24.290
We have decided to settle."

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:25.740
- Wow, that's so cool.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.960
I think it's going to be, like
if she presents that letter

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.140
and it's entered into evidence,

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:32.270
it's either going to be clearly her fault

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.650
because the manufacturer
did reach out many times

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:36.990
and she just missed it,
not just a solo letter,

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:39.160
or it's completely the
manufacturer's fault

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.570
because sending one letter
like that is not sufficient

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:43.810
to say, "Hey, this hip has
completely malfunctioning."

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:46.660
- I would really like to
see the text messages.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.807
Some person at the Peterson Institute,

00:18:48.807 --> 00:18:50.790
"Hey Dr. Torres."

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.250
- Gonna insert this real quick.

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:54.188
- Hip recalled, LOL.

00:18:54.188 --> 00:18:56.870
(laughing)

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:59.960
- [Narrator] Can an apology
actually heal our wounds?

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:00.793
- Not literally.

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:07.860
♪ Someone to save her
from her dying self ♪

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:09.212
- [Narrator] Ease our pain?

00:19:09.212 --> 00:19:14.212
♪ Always taking 10 steps
back on one step forward ♪

00:19:14.487 --> 00:19:15.980
♪ She's tired ♪

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:17.237
- Wait, what?

